WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.000
Some of the steps Joaquin Phoenix took to
become Gotham's Clown Prince of Crime seem

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.320
pretty risky, which is par for the course
when you're an actor of his caliber.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.160
But what, exactly, did Phoenix do to prepare
for such a disturbing role?

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.320
Here's how the celebrated performer transformed
himself into the Joker.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.759
Joaquin Phoenix says he didn't study any previous
film incarnations of the Joker in preparation,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.560
and that may be technically true.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.600
But there's one silent film he did study that's
essentially a Joker origin story.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:36.560
At the 2019 Venice Film Festival, Joker director
Todd Phillips said one of the movies he and

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.890
Phoenix found particularly important for Joker
prep was the 1928 silent film The Man Who

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:42.460
Laughs.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:47.180
Based on the Victor Hugo novel of the same
name, the film is about a man who has been

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.840
disfigured with a permanent smile since birth,
and becomes a sideshow attraction.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:58.040
The Man Who Laughs is often cited as the inspiration
for the Joker, who premiered in 1940's Batman

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:58.600
#1.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.290
DC comic book writer Grant Morrison, in fact,
called the Joker a direct lift from The Man

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.080
Who Laughs in his 2011 book Supergods.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.300
He goes so far to say it's a wonder how Joker's
creators got away with copying the film.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.940
So in a sense, even though the Jokers of Heath
Ledger or Jack Nicholson weren't considered

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.680
in the creation of Arthur Fleck, you could
argue Philips and Phoenix studied every Joker

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.620
by skipping them all and going straight to
the source.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.800
Philips has been clear he doesn't want anyone
to go into Joker thinking they're going to

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:34.600
be seeing the adaptation of a particular comic,
or that they should expect much inspiration

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.640
from the comics at all.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:40.340
But there's at least one way Arthur Fleck's
character mirrors the Joker of the comics

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.420
perhaps more closely than any of the film
adaptations we've seen so far.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:50.140
The Joker is almost always depicted as being
very skinny in the comics, sometimes impossibly

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:50.640
so.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.200
According to Phoenix, the first thing he did
to prepare for Joker was to lose weight — which

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.540
had a welcome side effect.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.220
He said at the Venice Film Festival,

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.360
"The first thing for us was the weight loss
— I think that's really what I started with

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.440
[...] As it turns out, that then affects your
psychology.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.820
You start to go mad when you lose that amount
of weight in that amount of time."

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.340
While The Man Who Laughs came out in 1928,
other films influencing Joker came from a

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.080
very different era — specifically, the '70s
and early '80s.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.240
That probably isn't a surprise.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.700
As soon as the first Joker trailer was released,
fans and critics saw the similarities to specific

00:02:26.709 --> 00:02:31.659
'70s films, particularly those directed by
acclaimed director Martin Scorsese — who

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:36.629
was originally attached as an executive producer
for Joker but had to bow out due to conflicting

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:37.220
commitments.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:44.180
Phillips named 1976's Taxi Driver and 1982's
King of Comedy as some of his chief inspirations

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.100
for Joker, both of which star Robert De Niro.

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:52.080
King of Comedy, like Joker, follows a failed
and mentally ill stand-up comic, played by

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:52.800
De Niro.

00:02:53.329 --> 00:02:58.060
In fun house mirror style, De Niro pops up
in Joker as an established talk show host

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.500
facing a mentally ill comedian of his own.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.560
For other inspirations, Phillips also mentioned
1973's Serpico and 1975's One Flew Over the

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:12.020
Cuckoo's Nest — a highlight in the career
of one of the most memorable movie Jokers,

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.099
Jack Nicholson.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.240
"How about it, ya creeps! You lunatics mental defectives ”

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.000
Films weren't the only media Joaquin Phoenix
studied to become the Joker.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.569
At the Venice Film Festival, he mentioned
reading a book that broke down the personality

00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:29.600
types of political assassins.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.900
We don't know exactly which books he read
because he didn't offer specifics, though

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:40.080
one likely candidate might be James W. Clarke's
1990 book On Being Mad or Merely Angry, which

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.639
among other things looks at John Hinckley's
1981 assassination attempt on President Ronald

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:46.140
Reagan.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:51.260
Regardless of exactly what Phoenix read, the
subject is telling in terms of the departure

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.820
from previous incarnations of the Joker.

00:03:54.109 --> 00:03:58.720
While the Joker of the comics and other films
would likely have few qualms about killing

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.380
politicians, it's not his primary focus.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.420
It also speaks to just how strongly the films
Phillips mentioned will influence Joker.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.100
Political assassination is very much on Travis
Bickle's mind in 1976's Taxi Driver.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:17.240
And the early '80s setting of Joker places
it right around the time Hinckley's own mental

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.129
illness drove him to try to assassinate President
Reagan.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.720
In Venice, Phoenix talked about how writing
a journal for Arthur Fleck helped him begin

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:26.860
to form the character.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:31.580
He told reporters that Todd Phillips sent
him a blank notebook to act as Fleck's journal

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.040
and joke diary, saying,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.840
"That was really helpful because I had been
there for a couple of weeks and wasn't sure

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.200
how I was going to start.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.700
I didn't know what to write, so I asked [Phillips]
for some suggestions, and after a few days,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.699
I ignored his suggestions and suddenly it
was coming out.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.860
It became a really important part of the discovery
of the character."

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:54.199
We may have already gotten a glimpse at Phoenix's
journal, or perhaps just a version inspired

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.400
by it, in the first Joker trailer.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.020
Phoenix says one of the things that makes
his take on the Joker feel so fresh has to

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:06.419
do with his approach to the character as a
persona that already exists within Arthur

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.060
Fleck just fighting to emerge.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:12.740
The actor said one of the earliest things
he tackled was the madman's infamous laugh.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.860
According to Phoenix, it wasn't easy to nail
down.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.820
He told the press in Venice,

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:22.260
"Before I even read the script, Todd came
over and talked me through what he wanted

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.539
out of this character and this movie […] and
he described the laughter as something that

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:30.569
was almost painful [...] Honestly, I didn't
think that I could do it.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:35.080
I would practice alone and then asked Todd
to come over to audition my laugh, because

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:38.400
I felt like I had to do it on the spot and
in front of somebody else.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:39.840
It took me a long time."

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.440
Reviewing the final trailer with Phoenix's
words in mind is interesting.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.099
During his confrontation with Thomas Wayne,
you can hear what Phoenix is talking about

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.700
as far as the laughter being "almost painful."

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:52.780
"ha."

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:58.700
Earlier, when he laughs loudly and then abruptly
stops, it sure sounds like a moment of the

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.660
Joker "trying to emerge."

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:04.820
"haha."

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.720
See the fruit of Phoenix's twisted labor when
Joker hits theaters October 4th, 2019.

